      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to set up some reorder logic for my shop. we sell small electronics components and i'm worried about running out of lithium battery cells during our peak season. can you give me a basic formula or some rules to help me figure out how much safety stock i should keep on hand to avoid stockouts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Reorder logic, a crucial aspect of inventory management, especially when dealing with perishable items like lithium battery cells. You can use the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) formula to determine your safety stock needs. The EOQ formula is: \n\nEOQ = √(2 × Demand × Cost of Inventory)\n\nWhere:\n- EOQ = Economic Order Quantity\n- Demand = Average monthly demand for lithium battery cells\n- Cost of Inventory = Total cost of inventory, including holding costs and ordering costs\n\nHowever, since you're concerned about running out of stock during peak season, you'll want to consider the lead time and the risk of stockouts. A common approach is to use the Safety Stock Formula:\n\nSafety Stock = (Lead Time × Demand) / (Service Level - 1)\n\nWhere:\n- Safety Stock = Amount of inventory to hold in reserve to meet demand\n- Lead Time = Time it takes to receive a new shipment of lithium battery cells\n- Demand = Average monthly demand for lithium battery cells\n- Service Level = Desired level of service, e.g., 95% (meaning you want to meet 95% of customer demand)\n\nTo calculate the safety stock, you'll need to determine your lead time and service level. For",
      "source": "target",
